ions from aqueous solutions [30,38–41]. However, the adsorbents mentioned above have some drawbacks: low selectivity towards fluoride ions, regeneration difficulties, low adsorption capacity, small specific surface area, and low porosity. Thus, developing an efficient adsorbent material with high adsorption capacity, facile regeneration, and low production cost for the efficient elimination of fluoride ions from the water remains a challenge requiring further investigation.

As a highly crystalline class of newly developed porous nanomaterials, MOFs are considered promising porous materials for removing fluoride ions from water [44,45]. Compared to the traditional adsorbent materials, the interest in MOF-based adsorbent materials is due to their exceptional surface area, various functional sites, and tunable pore architectures [46,47]. These outstanding properties make such materials promising candidates for a wide range of applications, including gas storage/separation, catalysis, drug adsorption, drug delivery, sensor, heavy metal adsorption/reduction, ion exchange, organic pollutants adsorption/degradation, etc. [6,47–57]. Moreover, the unique properties of MOFs such as high chemical activities, a broad range of functionalities, numerous active sites, high stability, tunable pore architectures make them potential candidates for simultaneous detection and removal of fluoride ions from water. Therefore, several MOFs

have been explored and used to detect and remove fluoride ions from water.

In this review, besides maximum adsorption capacity, partition coefficient parameter (PC) is used to better compare the various MOFs' adsorption performance towards fluoride because PC is independent of initial fluoride concentrations. PC can be calculated through the following equation [58]:

\[
PC=\frac{q_e}{C_e}
\]

where \( q_{\mathrm{e}} \) (mg/g) is the equilibrium adsorption capacity of fluoride ions onto the adsorbents and \( C_{\mathrm{e}} \) (mg/L) is the equilibrium fluoride concentration in the solution.

The present review systematically analyzes the recent developments of MOF-based materials to detect fluoride ions and selectively remove fluoride ions from aqueous solutions. The topics are categorized into fluoride detection and fluoride removal sections. The general design principles and applications of different MOF-based sensors and MOF-based adsorbents to detect and remove fluoride ions from aqueous solutions will be reviewed in detail. Finally, the outlook and challenges of developing MOF-based materials for fluoride detection and removal will be discussed.

# 2. Detection of fluoride ion

To detect the fluoride ions based on optical responses, usually, nanomaterials act as signal generators, signal carriers, signal regulators, or signal catalysts. Accordingly, fluorescent nanomaterials like MOFs act as a signal generator for the selective detection of fluoride ions based on the change of optical signals originating from fluoride ions. The fluoride detection fluorescent sensors mainly show three responsive modes: fluorescence turn-off, fluorescence turn-on, and ratiometric response [14]. In fluorescence turn-off responsive mode, fluoride ions can quench or reduce MOF nanoparticles' fluorescence via the inner filter effect or photoinduced electron transfer (Fig. 1a). The quenching effects occur due to the formation of various interactions between fluoride ions and the MOF nanoparticles. It was reported that in the inner filter effect, new complexes or molecules were produced due to the interaction or reaction of fluoride ions with other molecules. Under this condition, absorption spectra of the new materials might overlap with fluorescence emission or excitation spectra of MOF-based fluorescent materials; thereby, fluorescent of MOF nanoparticles is quenched due to the inner filter effect. In the photo-induced elec

