tron transfer mechanism, interaction or reaction of the fluoride ions with various functional groups located onto the surface of MOF nanoparticles may create the desired electron acceptors that can capture the excited electrons of the MOF-based fluorescent materials, leading to fluorescence turn-off. Since the fluorescent intensity can be disturbed by environmental interference, the result obtained by the aforementioned fluorescence turn-off sensors might not be too precise.

In fluorescence turn-on responsive mode, fluoride ions can turn on or enhance MOF nanoparticles' fluorescence by creating interactions with quenching agents or decomposition of MOF nanoparticles (Fig. 1b). Thus, it can be expected that this mode is more precise than that of fluorescence turn-off mode. In the case of ratio-metric mode, there are two fluorescence signals in the sensor. Accordingly, fluoride ions can significantly reduce or enhance one of these fluorescence signals, and the other constant signal acts as the reference signal. So, the ratio of these fluorescence signals can better determine the concentration of fluoride ions in the solutions (Fig. 1c). The ratiometric mode could provide the highest accuracy among fluorescence modes, mainly due to reference signals' existence in its detection mechanisms.

# 2.1. Lanthanide-based MOFs

Among the numerous developed MOFs, lanthanide-based MOFs (Ln-MOFs) are the best candidates for fluoride ion detection, thanks to their excellent luminescence properties, large Stokes shift, long decay lifetime, undisturbed emissive energy, and bright

visible emission, originating from the 4f electron shell [59,60]. Ln-MOFs' organic and inorganic compounds can provide suitable platforms to produce visible luminescence when induced by UV irradiation. Moreover, different sensitizing chromophores have been chosen to coordinate with Ln ions and then excite them, so a broad spectrum from blue to infrared light can be observed [61].

\(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) lanthanide ions have shown great potential to serve as luminescent centers. They have been widely used for fabricating fluorescence-based MOFs for fluoride detection. For instance, Chen and coworkers [62] fabricated Tb-based MOF (MOF-76), with the formula of Tb(BTC),G (BTC: benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylate, G: guest solvent like DMF (dimethylformamide) and MeOH (methanol)), for sensing of the fluoride ions in methanol solution. The prepared MOF was immersed in a methanol solution containing various anions, including Cl-, F-, Br-, \(S0_{4}^{2-}\) , and \(C0_{3}^{2-}\) . The results indicated that this MOF showed the highest turn-on response for fluoride ions. It was found that by immersing the MOF-76-MeOH into the methanol solution containing fluoride ions, the luminescence intensity relatively increased. Accordingly, the luminescence intensity of this MOF in the presence of fluoride ions was approximately four times stronger than the solution without fluoride ions. However, by repeating this experiment for MOF-76-DMF, the luminescence intensity enhancement was only two times stronger than the solution without fluoride ions. This observation might be due to the weaker interaction of fluoride ions with DMF molecules and the lower sensitivity of \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{O}\) and \(\mathsf{C{-}H}\) bonds of DMF molecules than the \(_{\mathrm{0-H}}\) bond of MeOH. It was suggested that the enhancement in the luminescence response of

