In another work, Hinterholzinger et al. [71] covalently functionalized \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Al) with fluorescein 5(6)-isothiocyanate (FITC) as a fluorescent reporter agent to improve its selectivity and sensitivity towards fluoride ions. A remarkable reduction of fluorescence intensity after the \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Al) functionalization with fluorescein was attributed to the quenching effect of fluorescein, fluorescein interactions with the metal center of MOF, the aromatic structure of the organic ligand, and the amino groups of the organic ligand. It was demonstrated that the detection of fluoride ions by this MOF is due to the MOF structure's disintegration, leading to FITC molecules' release into the solution and then a turn-on response. They reported that coordination bonding between Al ions of MOF and fluoride ions was the primary mechanism for the sensing process, based on the NMR titration results. The linear correlation in the range of 15–1600 ppb was reported for fluoride detection. More importantly, this MOF showed high selectivity towards fluoride ions in the existence of other interfering anions such as \(\mathrm{Br}^{-}\), \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\), \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\), \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\), \(\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}\), and \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO}^{-}\).

The encapsulation of fluorescence \(2^{\prime},7^{\prime}\)-dichlorofluorescein (DCF) into the porous structure of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Al) was followed to improve its sensitivity to fluoride ions [74]. The modified MOF was decomposed in the presence of fluoride ions due to the creation of strong binding energy (coordination bonds) between fluoride ions and aluminum ions components of MOF. The aluminum hexafluoride \((\mathrm{[AlF_{6}]^{3-}})\) complex ions were formed due to

Fluoride-MOF interaction, as shown in Fig. 6a. The free DCF molecules in the solution captured the generated energy from the chemical reaction between \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and bis(2,4,6-trichlorophenyl)oxalate (TCPO) molecules and showed strong chemiluminescence emission (Fig. 6b). The chemiluminescence signal was significantly influenced by the concentration of fluoride ions and exhibited a relatively linear response in a wide range of fluoride concentrations (9.5 ppb – 1.52 ppm). The detection limit of this modified MOF towards fluoride ions was 0.95 ppb [74], lower than the national standard criterion and the previously reported materials, indicating their bright future in fluoride detection.

# 2.3. Zirconium-based MOFs

The Zr-based MOFs, especially the UiO-66 series, have shown great promise for fluoride detection due to their high porosity and robust structure, stemming from the presence of strong coordination bonds between the highly stable secondary building units (SBU) of \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}\) and BDC linkers [90–94]. For instance, Zhu et al. [15] used an amino-functionalized UiO-66 MOF (\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66) as a receptor for detecting fluoride because of its luminescence properties, permanent porosity, and the presence of open sites that can bind with fluoride ions. A highly sensitive and selective fluorescent signal was observed for the detection of fluoride ions in aqueous solutions. As can be observed in Fig. 7, the detection of fluoride ions was not affected by the interference of cations and anions that existed in the solution, including humic acid, metal ions, \(\mathrm{Br}^{-}\), \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\), \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\), \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\), \(\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}\), \(\mathrm{CO}_{3}^{2-}\), and \(\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}\). A detection limit of
