In another attempt, a modified Zr-based MOF was designed by incorporating fluorescein sodium (FS) within the framework of UiO-66 via an in-situ synthesis technique [27]. The prepared modified UiO-66 MOF (FS@UiO-66) was a highly sensitive and selective chemical sensor with quick response time and naked-eye recognition to detect fluoride ions in aqueous solutions. During sensing, interactions between fluoride ions and zirconium ions of the modified MOF resulted in the decomposition of the host framework (FS@UiO-66) along with the release of FS molecules in the solution, which caused a turn-on response. The fluorescence signal was dependent on fluoride concentration and exhibited an excellent linear correlation over a wide range of fluoride concentrations \((0{-}7.6\ \mathrm{ppm})\) with the detection limit of 83.6 ppb. Since fluoride ions selectively reacted with this framework, FS@UiO-66 was highly selective towards fluoride detection, even in the coexistence of other onions.

Recently, a turn-on fluorescent sensor based on Terbium \((\mathrm{Tb}^{3+})\) post-coordination modified Zr-based MOF was fabricated via mixed ligands (terephthalic acid and isophthalic acid), possessing uncoordinated carbonyl groups due to the asymmetrical structure of isophthalic acid [16]. It was found that the crystalline structure of modified MOF \((\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}@\mathrm{Zr-MOF})\) was similar to unmodified Zr-MOF, while its luminescence properties were remarkably improved. The reported MOF showed a high potential to detect fluoride ions with good selectivity, high sensitivity, excellent stability, and a low detection limit. The fluoride ion detection limit of 0.35 ppm was reported, much lower than the maximum recommended level \((1.5\,\mathsf{p p m})\) in drinking water. More importantly, modified MOF showed a recognizable luminescence response to fluoride ions in the presence of other interfering anions due to Lewis acid-base interactions of these ions with the Zr center of the modified MOF [16].

# 2.4. Other MOFs

Organic linkers have a dominant role in the fluoride detection of MOFs. Among the abundant reported organic ligands, nitrogen-donor organic ligands are the potential candidates due to nitrogen atoms' ability to coordinate with numerous metal ions. The presence of nitrogen atoms within MOFs' structure can promote intermolecular hydrogen bonds with different anions, rendering a cornerstone for sensing anions [62]. Following this strategy, Chen et al. [77] synthesized a novel Cu-MOF \(\mathrm{[Cu_{4}I(T I P E)_{3}]}31\) based on \({\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}\) and tetra(3-imidazophenyl)ethylene) (TIPE) via a simple solvothermal method in the presence of sulfosalicylic acid. It was reported that Cu-MOF showed blue emission that exhibited a highly selective and sensitive response to the fluoride ions via fluorescence turn-off responsive mechanism, which was attributed to the substitution of iodine anion by the fluoride anion and the formation of hydrogen bonds between fluoride ions and MOF. The detection limit of this MOF towards fluoride ions was 40.09 ppb, with a linear correlation covering a range of 190–1330 ppb. However, it was found that the presence of other halogen ions (Br and Cl) at high concentrations could interfere with the detection of fluoride ions. Similarly, Wentz and Campbell [96] synthesized MOFs (M–NDISA, \(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{N}\mathbf{i}\) , Mg) based on \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}/\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}\) as metal centers and \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{NDISA}\) as redox-active naphthalene diimide-based organic ligands (Fig. 10). In the presence of fluoride ions, solution color rapidly changed due to strong interactions between fluoride ions and M–NDISA frameworks.

More recently, a rare-earth MOF (RE-MOFs, named as YTMNs) was successfully synthesized using yttrium \((\mathsf{Y}^{3+})\) ions as rare-earth metal centers and 4,4,4,4-(porphine-5,10,15,20-tetrayl) tetrakis (benzoic acid) (TCCP) as the porphyrin-based planar organic ligand via coordination chelation (Fig. 11a) [78]. The high coordination number of RE metal ions produced many coordinative unsaturated metal centers. These centers and other functional groups onto the surface of RE-MOFs pores could enhance RE-MOFs' detection performance through acid-base interactions, coordination reaction, and electrostatic interactions. Pure TCCP usually shows a bright red fluorescence peak at \(645~\mathrm{nm}\) . The fluorescence emission was quenched after preparing YTMNs nanoplates due to the photo-induced electron transfer effect. However, as a consequence of distinctive host-guest interactions between the fluoride ions and YTMNs framework, the fluorescence of YTMNs was lighted in a solution pH lower than 6 (Fig. 11b), demonstrating them as a fluorescence nanosensor for fluoride ion detection with high selectivity and sensitivity. Additionally, the fluorescence signal of MOF was dependent on fluoride concentration and exhibited an excellent linear correlation of fluoride concentrations between 0.05 and 8 ppm with a detection limit of 8.5 ppb. The alkaline solutions \((\mathsf{p H}>7)\) could enhance the fluorescence of YTMNs, and fluo
