# 3. Removal of fluoride ion from water

Several studies have demonstrated that high-valent cations such as Fe(III), Zr(IV), Ce(III), and La(III) have a superior ability to bind with fluoride ions via strong Lewis acid-base interactions. The traditional adsorbents suffer several drawbacks. For instance, lanthanum-based adsorbents are costly, or alumina-based adsorbents are unstable in alkaline solutions and show leakage risk. Unlike the traditional adsorbents, MOFs have shown a high adsorption ability towards fluoride ions, mainly due to their large available surface area, ultrahigh porosity, numerous active sites, excellent stability in a wide range of pH, and tailorable functionalities [14,97].

# 3.1. Pure MOFs

MOF stability against water and fluoride ions can determine its defluoridation performance. In this regard, the stability of eleven well-known MOFs against fluoride solutions was investigated by Zhong et al. [44] to evaluate their stability. The fluoride solutions (concentration of \(50{-}100~\mathrm{ppm}\)) containing each MOF (dosage of \(200\,\mathrm{ppm}\)) were stirred at \(303\,\mathrm{K}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\). Then, MDR was performed, and it was observed that the crystalline structures of MIL-53(M) \(\left(\mathbf{M}\ =\ \mathbf{F}\mathbf{e}\right.\) and \(\mathbf{Cr}\)) remained unchanged in fluoride solution \((100~\mathrm{\ppm})\). In contrast, MIL-53(Al) particles were dissolved in fluoride solution and completely disappeared after 2 days due to different metal activities \(\left(\mathsf{A l}>\mathsf{C r}>\mathsf{F e}\right)\) [108]. Contrary to MIL-53 (Al), the MIL-68(Al) particles were dissolved entirely in fluoride solution in \(<\!1\,\mathrm{~h~}\), ascribed to differences in their pore topologies. The stability of CAU-1 \(\left(\left[\mathrm{Al}_{8}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}\right]_{8}(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{bdc})_{6}\right)\) was similar to MIL-53(Al), while CAU-6 \(\left(\left[\mathrm{Al}_{13}(\mathrm{OH})_{27}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\right.\) \(\left.\mathrm{bdc})_{3}(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{7}\mathrm{OH})_{6}\mathrm{Cl}_{6}\right]\right)\) was highly stable against fluoride solution, mainly due to the presence of more \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and –OH groups that protect the Al center by forming hydrogen bonds with fluoride ions (H-O-H. . .F and –O-H. . .F).

Zr- and Hf-based MOFs are more stable than Al-based MOFs, despite their similar metal activity due to the differences in their coordination numbers. In UiO-66(Zr), each \(\mathrm{Zr}^{4+}\) is eight-coordinated, and each cluster is connected to twelve organic ligands, while Al-MOFs are hexa-coordinated [52,91,99]. It was observed that among the MOFs, UiO-66 showed the best stability in fluoride solutions and exhibited the highest adsorption capacity \((44.92~\mathrm{mg/g})\) for fluoride ions (Table 3). The experimental fluoride adsorption data of UiO-66(Zr) was well described by the pseudo-second-order kinetics and Langmuir isotherm models. Increasing the number of hydroxyl groups was suggested as a beneficial strategy to improve the fluoride adsorption performance of UiO-66, probably due to their ability to form hydrogen bonds with fluoride ions. The authors claimed that the stability of MOFs in fluoride solutions depends on (i) the activity of the metal center, (ii) pore topology, (iii) coordination number, and (iv) hydrophobicity.

Some contradictory results were reported by Kamarehie et al. [100], where UiO-66 and ZIF-8 were employed to remove fluoride ions from water. It was found that UiO-66 \((20\,\mathrm{mg/g})\) showed lower adsorption capacity compared to ZIF-8 \((2.5~\mathrm{mg/g})\), possibly due to larger BET (Brunauer–Emmett–Teller) surface area \((1050~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})\) and total pore volume \((0.57\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})\) of ZIF-8. The isotherm and kinetics of fluoride adsorption were described by the Langmuir isotherm and pseudo-second-order kinetic models. In another study, the effects of various parameters such as adsorbent dosage, contact

time, stirring rate, pH, and temperature on the adsorption capacity of fluoride ions onto ZIF-8 were investigated [101]. Optimum conditions for the adsorption of fluoride ions onto ZIF-8 nanoparticles were introduced at the adsorbent dosage of \(60~\mathrm{ppm}\), contact time of \(15~\mathrm{min}\), stirring speed of \(400~\mathrm{rpm}\), pH of 8, and temperature of \(20~^{\circ}\!C\).

Zhang et al. [102] employed MIL-96(Al) with the formula of \(\mathrm{Al_{12}O(O H)_{18}(H_{2}O)_{3}(A l_{2}(O H)_{4})/(B T C)_{6}{\cdot}24H_{2}O}\) for removing fluoride ions from water. MIL-96(Al) showed better performance over a wide range of pHs from 3 to 10. The fluoride adsorption performance was not considerably changed in the presence of other anions such as chloride, sulfate, phosphate, nitrate, and bicarbonate. It was demonstrated that \(\mathsf{A l}^{3+}\) residual and fluoride removal efficiency was superior to nanoalumina or activated alumina. The Langmuir isotherm model was well fitted to adsorption data, and a maximum fluoride adsorption capacity of about \(32~\mathrm{{mg/g}}\) at \(298\,\mathrm{~K~}\) was reported for the MOF. The adsorption kinetics data of fluoride ions onto MIL-96 followed the pseudo-second-order kinetics model. The column tests were also performed using the natural groundwater to estimate the MOF's ability in defluoridation; the results illustrated MIL-96 potential as an excellent adsorbent for removing fluoride ions from drinking water [103].

In a similar work, Wang et al. [104] employed MIL-96(Al), having a granular structure of rice with a surface area of \(220~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) to remove fluoride ions from water. It was found that initial pH did not significantly affect the removal performance. The adsorption capacity for fluoride ions was relatively constant in the pH range of 6–9; the Langmuir isotherm model well described the adsorption isotherm data. The theoretical adsorption capacity for fluoride ions was calculated to be higher than \(42~\mathrm{mg/g}\) at \(298\,\mathrm{~K~}\), much higher than the activated alumina \((0.8{-}2.0~\mathrm{mg/g})\) [105]. The NaOH solution regenerated MIL-96(Al) completely, and nanoparticles showed a good removal performance even after seven adsorption-desorption cycles.

The Ce-based MOF (Ce-bpdc MOF, where \(1,1^{\prime}\)-biphenyl- \(^{4,4^{\prime}}\)-dicarboxylic acid is bpdc) exhibited a maximum fluoride adsorption capacity of \(45.5~\mathrm{mg/g}\) at room temperature and neutral pH \((\approx\!7)\) [106], which showed \(>80\%\) removal efficiency for fluoride ions removal with a PC value of \(1.54~\mathrm{L/g}\). The experimental adsorption data were well fitted to the Langmuir isotherm model, indicating that the adsorption of fluoride ions was monolayer. The actual groundwater testing demonstrated the high potential of Ce-bpdc MOF as an effective adsorbent. Another novel Ce-MOF (Ce(BTC) \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}0\right)_{6}\)) exhibited a maximum adsorption capacity of \(181.8~\mathrm{mg/g}\) for fluoride ions at \(\mathsf{p H}\,6.5\) [107], where the adsorption of fluoride ions onto Ce-MOFs was rapid at the beginning, followed by slower adsorption with about \(45\%\) adsorption after \(1\ \mathrm{min}\).

Ma et al. [46] synthesized Ce-based MOF \(\left([\mathrm{Ce}(\mathrm{L}_{1})_{2.5}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})\mathrm{H}_{2^{-}}\right.\) \(\left.\mathrm{O}\right)_{2}\right]2\mathrm{DMF}\) and compared its fluoride adsorption performance with lanthanide-based MOF \(\left([\mathrm{Eu}_{3}(\mathrm{L}_{2})_{2}(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{DMF})_{0.22}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{5.78}\right]\). guest). The Ce-MOF showed higher adsorption capacity \((103.95~\mathrm{mg/g})\) and faster adsorption rate \((1.79~\mathrm{g/mg~min})\) than Eu-MOF \((57.01~\mathrm{mg/g})\), suggesting higher interactions between fluoride ions and Ce sites than the Eu sites. The effect of solution pH on adsorption was investigated by changing the solution pH from 2 to 10, and higher adsorption capacities were observed for both MOFs at lower pH (3–7). The adsorption capacities decreased significantly at pH higher than 8, due to the adsorption competition between fluoride ions and hydroxyl ions. At lower pH, these MOFs were positively charged. Hence, nucleophilic replacement of hydroxyl groups with fluoride ions was quick, whereas such replacement was not possible at higher pH due to the abundance of hydroxyl groups in the solution. However, under strongly acidic conditions \((\mathsf{p H}\approx2)\), the structure of MOFs might partially collapse, resulting in the reduction of their fluoride adsorption performances. The adsorption capacity of fluoride onto these MOFs
