Quite recently, Ghosh and Das [108] synthesized water-stable MOF via green solvothermal method using Sn(II) as the metal center and benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylic acid (TMA) as the organic

ligand, which offered a high fluoride removal efficiency (84%) in the pH range of 3–10, indicating its superior structural stability. Langmuir isotherm model described the equilibrium adsorption of fluoride ions onto Sn(II)-TMA-MOF, and its theoretical fluoride adsorption capacity was 30.85 mg/g at room temperature. Experi-
mental adsorption data followed both pseudo-first-order and pseudo-second-order kinetic models, suggesting that fluoride adsorption occurred via physisorption and chemisorption processes. Furthermore, thermodynamic studies indicated that adsorption was spontaneous, endothermic, and thermodynamically feasible, and adsorption performance remained unaffected in the co-existence of interfering anions such as \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\), \(\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}\), and \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\). In the presence of \(\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}\) fluoride, the removal efficiency of Sn (II)-TMA-MOF was slightly reduced at the high pH of the \(\mathrm{NaH}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}\) solution. Deprotonation occurred on the surface of Sn(II)-TMA-MOF due to electrostatic repulsion between fluoride ions and the negatively charged adsorbent surface [109]. The authors proposed that electrostatic repulsion, high pH, and competitive adsorption together led to a reduction in fluoride removal efficiency in the presence of \(\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}\).

# 3.2. Modified MOFs

Engineering the MOFs structure using various ligands and postsynthetic approaches for the targeted application is highly promising. Using amino-functionalized ligands in the structure of UiO-66 was beneficial to improved fluoride adsorption due to the ability of amino functional groups to create strong electrostatic interactions with fluoride. Based on this strategy, Lin et al. [110] synthesized a UiO-66 MOF with \(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) group on its organic ligands. The modified MOF had a similar structure to UiO-66, but its crystalline structure did not change in the presence of fluoride. The modified MOF exhibited a relatively constant adsorption capacity for fluoride ions in the presence of other ions and acidic solutions. It was also observed that \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) \((52.6~\mathrm{mg/g})\) had a higher adsorption capacity for fluoride ions compared to un-functionalized UiO-66 MOF \((41.2~\mathrm{mg/g})\), suggesting strong interactions between fluoride ions and Zr sites of \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\). Similar results were also reported

by Massoudinejad et al. [111] by using amino-functionalized UiO-66 (UiO-66-amine) to remove fluoride ions from water.

Jeyaseelan and coworkers [112] synthesized two MOFs based on \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) as a metal center and BDC (benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid) and ABDC (2-amino benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid) as organic ligands. Fe-based MOFs: equilibrium fluoride adsorption data were well fitted to Langmuir isotherm, for which maximum adsorption capacities of fluoride onto Fe@BDC and Fe@ABDC were calculated to be 4.90 and \(4.92~\mathrm{mg/g}\), respectively. The kinetic studies revealed that intra-particle diffusion and pseudo-second-order kinetic models were applicable for describing fluoride removal. The authors suggested that the adsorption mechanisms of fluoride ions onto Fe-based MOFs were mainly due to electrostatic interactions and complexation (Fig. 12).

Similarly, using BDC and ABDC as organic ligands, the authors synthesized two new lanthanum (La)-based MOF, i.e., La@BDC MOF and La@ABDC MOF [113]. The maximum adsorption capacity of La@BDC MOF and La@ABDC MOF was 4.92 and \(4.95~\mathrm{mg/g}\), respectively. The electrostatic interactions and complexation were suggested as the main defluoridation mechanisms of the La-based MOFs. Amino-functionalized MIL-53(Al)–\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) with hierarchical hollow morphology was another MOF employed for removing fluoride ions from water [114]. The adsorption performance of MIL-53(Al)–\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) towards fluoride was investigated over a wide pH range (1–12). The highest fluoride adsorption was strongly dependent on pH. In contrast, the lowest adsorption of \(\approx50\%\) at pH 7 was due to strong electrostatic interactions between fluoride ions and positively charged MIL-53(Al)–\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), accounting for high adsorption at pH 1–3. Equilibrium adsorption of fluoride ions onto MIL-53(Al)–\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) followed Langmuir isotherm model, indicating monolayer adsorption with maximum adsorption of \(1070.6~\mathrm{mg/g}\). As shown in Table 3, the fluoride adsorption capacity of MIL-53(Al)–\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) was much higher than those of reported adsor

