Previous findings showed that the adsorption of fluoride by cerium (Ce) is because of its strong affinity to fluoride ions [115]. A large body of literature on Ce-containing adsorbents [116–119] has shown their good adsorption performance for fluoride ions. However, they were restricted to operate at low pH values. In a study, Ce was doped into the MIL-96(Al) porous structure to overcome this challenge [120]. The maximum adsorption capacity of Ce-doped MIL-96(Al) for fluoride ions was found to be \(38.65~\mathrm{mg/}\) g at \(298~\mathrm{K}\). However, by evaluating the contribution of MIL-96(Al) in fluoride adsorption, the maximum adsorption capacity of Ce ions for fluoride was \(296.75~\mathrm{mg/g}\), revealing that defluoridation efficiency of Ce ions in Ce-doped MIL-96(Al) is nearly ten-times that for Ce-mZrP [119], six-times that for \(\mathsf{C e}_{2}0_{3}\) [118], and two-times that in Ce-Mn oxides [118]. Fluoride adsorption performance of Ce-doped MIL-96(Al) remained relatively constant in a wide range of pH of 3–10. The residual of Al and Ce ions was in the acceptable range for drinking water.

Moreover, the presence of nitrate, chloride, bicarbonate, phosphate, and sulfate showed no considerable effect on the fluoride adsorption performance of Ce-doped MIL-96(Al). Increasing the concentration of competing \(\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}\) ion to 100 ppm reduced Ce-doped MIL-96(Al) fluoride removal efficiency from 92 to \(80\%\), showing its outstanding performance. The good adsorption performance of the MOF in the presence of competing anions was attributed to the unique size-selective ability of the micropores in the Ce-doped MIL-96(Al) structure.

# 3.3. Fumarate-based MOFs

The aluminum fumarate (AlFu) MOF showed excellent water stability, relatively superior to other reported MOFs [121], and its crystal structure was similar to MOF-53, consisting of numerous Al-OH-Al chains linked to fumarate ligands (Fig. 13) [122]. AlFu MOF has a robust 3D porous structure with square channels recently marketed as BasoliteTM A520 by BASF [123]. Karmaker and coworkers used this adsorbent for removing fluoride contamination from water [124] due to its superior chemical stability. The AlFu MOF has a microporous structure with a surface area of

\(1156\,\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) and an average pore diameter of \(17\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\). The thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) suggests its thermal stability of up to \(700~\mathrm{K}\). The adsorption capacity for fluoride ions was \(600\;\mathrm{mg/g}\) at \(\mathsf{p H}\,7\) and \(293~\mathrm{K}\). The adsorption capacity decreased with increasing temperature, suggesting an exothermic adsorption process. The adsorption process was described by the Freundlich isotherm model, suggesting heterogeneous multilayer adsorption. The substitution of hydroxyl ions \(\left(0\mathrm{H}^{+}\right)\)in AlFu MOF by fluoride ions was introduced as the primary adsorption mechanism.

A series of Fe-based MOFs such as MIL-88A, MIL-101, MIL-53, and MIL-100 was developed to remove fluoride ions from water [125]. MIL-88A showed the highest fluoride adsorption performance \((49.42~\mathrm{mg/g})\) for fluoride ions at room temperature. This MOF had a 3D porous network constructed from octahedral Fe and oxygen center dimer connected by fumaric acid ligands. Moreover, the co-existence of other interfering anions such as nitrate, sulfate, chloride, phosphate, and bicarbonate did not significantly influence the adsorption performance of MIL-88A towards fluoride ions, suggesting the unique size selectivity of micropores [125].

Zirconium fumarate-MOF (MOF-801) is another stable fumarate-based MOF that showed an excellent ability to remove fluoride ions from water with an adsorption capacity of \(40~\mathrm{mg/g}\) at \(303\mathrm{~K~}\) [109]. Its adsorption capacity remained high and stable over a wide range of pH (from 2 to 10). The adsorption capacity was not considerably affected by high fluoride concentration or the co-existence of other interfering anions (e.g., \(\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}\), \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\), and \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\)). The fluoride adsorption onto MOF-801 followed Langmuir isotherm and pseudo-second-order kinetic models, suggesting monolayer adsorption with chemisorption through the exchange of fluoride ions by \(-\mathrm{OH}\) groups within MOF-801. More importantly, the practical utilization of MOF-801 for removing fluoride from real water indicated its ability as an effective and promising adsorbent to remove fluoride ions from water [45]. The maximum removal efficiency at the equilibrium point was \(>92\%\), and the process followed pseudo-second-order kinetic models. Equilibrium adsorption data were fitted to the Langmuir isotherm model, and the maximum adsorption capacity was calculated to be \(19.42~\mathrm{mg/g}\) at room temperature.

The accumulation of fluoride ions in tea at concentrations of \(100{-}300~\mathrm{mg/kg}\) is generally considered safe. Still, in some special teas like brick tea, the fluoride concentration is usually at levels up to \(600~\mathrm{mg/kg}\), potentially harmful to humans [126]. Therefore,

