# 3.4. MOFs composites

In recent years, combining MOFs with other adsorbents has received much attention due to the synergistic effects of multiple functionalities in the composites compared to pure MOFs [128– 133]. The combination of MOFs and other adsorbent materials could improve the physico-chemical properties, morphology, stability, and adsorption kinetics of the composite [129,131]. For instance, Zhang et al. [134] synthesized a novel \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{HKUST}{-1}\) composite by growing a Cu-MOF shell around the \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) sphere core and used it to remove fluoride ions from water. The pore diameter of the HKUST-1 shell was \(0.52\ \mathrm{nm}\) smaller than the radius of various hydrated anions. The composite showed excellent selectivity for hydrated fluoride ions with a diameter of \(0.298\ \mathrm{nm}\) . Moreover, the recycling investigations suggested that the fluoride adsorption

performance was not reduced remarkably even after the seven adsorption-desorption cycles.

The Separation of powdery MOF nanoparticles from the treated water is a complex challenge, which has hindered the broad application of this type of adsorbent for water treatment [130]. A practical method to overcome this challenge is combining nanoparticles with other materials such as polymers, magnetic nanoparticles, etc., [5]. Based on this concept, Xie et al. [97] synthesized a new monolithic composite adsorbent by the in-situ growth of UiO-66 nanoparticles onto the porous carbon foam. The UiO-66/ CF composite showed rapid adsorption kinetics and high adsorption capacity \((\approx\!295~\mathrm{mg/g})\) for fluoride ions at room temperature and neutral pH \((\approx\!7)\) , remarkably higher than that of pure UiO-66 \((44.92\;\mathrm{mg/g})\) [44]. This was attributed to the presence of abundant appropriate active sites for mass transfer and good pore accessibility.

The adsorption performance of the UiO-66/CF composite was significantly influenced by the pH, as shown in Fig. 15a. The fluoride removal efficiency increased initially from \(\mathsf{p H}\ 3\) to 6 and reached the maximum in a pH range of 5–7. The removal efficiency was reduced with a further increase of pH and showed the lowest value of \(42\%\) at a pH of 11. At low pH (pH lower than the point of zero charge \(\left(\mathsf{p H}_{\mathrm{PZC}}\right)\) , the UiO-66/CF composite surface was positively charged, facilitating the adsorption of fluoride ions via electrostatic attraction. More hydrogen bonding under acidic solution was beneficial for ligand/anion-exchange reaction of M OH groups by the fluoride ions. As shown in Fig. 15a, fluoride removal of UiO66/CF composite enhanced with the increase of solution pH; however, the positive surface charge was reduced. Based on these

