On the contrary, at high pH \( (\mathrm{>pH}_{\mathrm{PZ}}) \), the composite surface tends to be deprotonated and get a more negative charge. It reduces fluoride removal efficiency due to strong electrostatic repulsion between fluoride ions and adsorbent surface [135]. Moreover, in this condition, excessive \( \mathrm{OH}^{-} \)ions might compete with fluoride ions to occupy the adsorption sites, reducing fluoride removal efficiency at higher pH. The Langmuir adsorption isotherm and pseudo-second-order kinetic models were fitted with the adsorption data, suggesting monolayer adsorption with chemisorption mechanism via the ligand exchange (Fig. 15b). Based on the suggested adsorption mechanism, the fluoride adsorption onto UiO-66/CF composite occurs via ion exchange interaction, electrostatic interactions, and complexation mechanisms. More interestingly, the column adsorption experiment demonstrated that UiO-66/CF composite had a high defluoridation performance in real groundwater.

In another study, alumina substrate and Zr-MOF nanoparticles were used to fabricate membranes for removing fluoride ions from drinking water [136]. Both membrane and pure Zr-MOF with a BET surface area of \( 740\,\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g} \) were applied to remove fluoride ions from water. An adsorption capacity of \( 102.40\,\mathrm{mg/g} \) was achieved at \( 318\,\mathrm{~K~} \) and neutral pH. The adsorption data were well described by the Langmuir isotherm model, suggesting a monolayer process. The adsorption of fluoride ions onto Zr-MOF reached equilibrium in only \( 20\;\mathrm{min} \). Pseudo-second-order kinetic model well described the kinetic behavior. The co-existence of other interfering ions showed a negligible effect on the adsorption performance, and stable adsorption was observed over a wide range of pH (3–9), much better than the reported adsorbents. The –OH groups located on the surface of the membrane and zirconium active sites were recognized as the primary sites for fluoride adsorption, which also controlled the overall performance of the prepared membranes. The defluoridation performance of Zr-MOF membranes was dependent on initial fluoride concentration and flow rate. The defluoridation ability of the membrane with a thickness of \( 20~{\upmu\mathrm{m}} \) changed from 5510 to \( 4664~\mathrm{~L/m}^{2} \) when fluoride concentration was increased from 5 to \( 10~\mathsf{p p m} \). The application of membranes and MOF-containing membranes has shown great potential for removing fluoride ions from water [137,138]. For instance, Karmakar et al. [139] fabricated a novel mixed-matrix membrane (MMM)

using cellulose acetate phthalate (CAP) polymer and aluminum fumarate-MOF (AlFu) for removing fluoride from the groundwater. The adsorption capacity increased from 107 to \( 179~\mathrm{{\mg/g}} \) by increasing MOF loadings from 2 to \( 10~\mathrm{wt}\% \). The fluoride rejection of specimen containing \( 10~\mathrm{wt\%} \) of AlFu(CAP/AlFu-10) was \( >99\% \). The electrostatic interactions between the positively charged CAP/AlFu-10 membrane and fluoride ions were the primary fluoride removal mechanism. The AlFu particles were firmly attached to the CAP matrix via hydrogen bonding. In a similar work, AlFu nanoparticles were loaded into the polyacrylonitrile (PAN) matrix to produce MMM hollow fiber membranes [140]. The fabricated membrane water permeability was enhanced from \( 3.5\,\times\,10^{-10} \) to \( 4.5\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm{mPa\,s} \) with increasing MOF loadings from 0 to \( 10\,\mathrm{wt} \) \( \% \) due to increased hydrophilicity. The fluoride adsorption capacity of the membranes was increased from 128 to \( 205~\mathrm{mg/g} \) by enhancing the MOF loadings from 3 to \( 10~\mathrm{wt\%} \), which was much higher than the reported membranes [141].

# 4. Simultaneous detection and removal of fluoride

In contrast to conventional adsorbing materials, MOF materials possess several benefits that can integrate sensing and adsorption in the matrix [158–160]. The most attractive advantage of MOF is the presence of organic ligands and metal clusters, which can be modulated to introduce favorable functionality to the framework [161]. These characteristics could facilitate the interactions between the target analytes and the MOF skeletons shifted the changes into the detectable signal in the luminescence [161], and integrated efficient adsorption performance of the target analytes [162]. Besides, the large surface area of MOF generates several opportunities for guest molecules to enter and interact with the framework, which is quite beneficial for the efficient removal of target analytes [163]. In particular, the permanent porous structure and abundant pores can promote fast diffusion and transportation of target analytes, leading to a short response time and rapid adsorption kinetics. More importantly, adsorption and preconcentration of target analytes within the porous structure of MOF will give rise to enhanced local concentration, resulting in detection sensitivity improvement [164].

Other unique features of MOFs, such as high chemical activity, a broad range of functionality, numerous active sites, high stability, and tunable pore architectures, make them potential candidates for simultaneous detection and removal of water-related pollutants [164–166]. Zhang et al. [166] fabricated a multifunctional UiO-66-based hybrid nanocomposite that simultaneously

